   131	It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
   132	
   133	She came down to Thornwater's eastern gate at dusk on the hundred and twenty-fifth day. The bar was drawn for her before she had called.
   134	
   135	"It's yours when you're here," said the steading's woman. She had a lantern, and she looked the healer over with it as she would have looked over a cart that was late home. "You're thin. You've been looked for."
   136	
   137	"By the bench?"
   138	
   139	"By the bench. And by one that walked."
   140	
   141	She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every evening that she could remember, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
   142	
   143	"Her arm's whole," said Oryn, upward. "It held a stair, which it shouldn't have. It's held nothing since."
   144	
   145	The two fingers lay down again on the wood.
   146	
   147	There was no brown mule at the trough. She had known that there would not be, and she looked for it all the same, and was annoyed with herself. The back room had been swept. Her strip of canvas lay along the sill as she had left it, and somebody had put a cold fowl on the plank under a cloth.
   148	
   149	There were seven of them under the back room's window in the morning, in the frost, and the first was sitting bolt upright on the bench's end with a stick across her knees.
   150	
   151	She was a very small old woman. Oryn had mended that knee at a mill, thirteen days before, and had seen its owner go away up a valley on a hurdle between two grandsons. She came over the low place every year before the snow, to her daughter's hearth in this long house; and Oryn had told her, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee here, after the thaw.
   152	
   153	"I said after the thaw," said Oryn.
   154	
   155	"You said it to a woman lying on a hurdle. I never agreed to it." The old woman rapped the knee with her knuckles. "I rested it ten days, as I was bid. Then I came over to my daughter's, as I do, and I came on my own two legs, to find out whether the thing was yours or mine. It's a day and a half. I did it in two. I'd one of the boys with me to carry the bread."
   156	
   157	"On a stick."
   158	
   159	"I never leaned on it. I brought it to hit him with."
   160	
   161	Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
   162	
   163	"It's held," she said.
   164	
   165	"I know that it's held. I've just walked a hill on it. I came to have you say so."
   166	
   167	That was one of seven. She did the rest as she always did them, and two of them took a mending apiece, and at the end of it she sat on her keg with her head down for a count of fifty.
   168	
   169	The word came on the second day, with the last of the grain carts.
   170	
   171	It was a long stooped carter whom she knew, fetching for the gates up the water, and he had been as far as the ford with the oxen and a gate beyond. He gave it to her in the yard, as he had been given it, in the order of the road. The mill had had nothing worse than a crushed thumb. The two gates above it had buried nobody. At the ford a man had lost a finger to a chain and kept the hand; they had bound it themselves, badly, and it had not gone bad. And at the gate where the little one was, the breathing was no different. She coughed at night, and slept after.
   172	
   173	Oryn heard the whole of it standing, with her hands under her arms.
   174	
   175	Three weeks, then, and seven gates, and nobody dead of it. She did not take that for a reason. A person who has crossed thin ice once has not learned that ice will bear; she has been lucky, and knows it by the cold in her stomach afterward. But the round was whole. Every gate on it was where she had left it, with everybody in it that she had left there, and waiting.
   176	
   177	She owed that bench two days more. Then she would go north by the wicket.
   178	
   179	---
   180	
   181	It was the steading's woman who told her about the carter, on the evening of the hundred and twenty-eighth, with the sky gone the colour of a slate.
   182	
   183	"He goes down to the board at first light, with my bark. He'll not come up again this side of the thaw, if that does what it looks like." The woman jerked her chin at the north. "If you've anything to send to that crew of yours, it's tonight."
   184	
   185	"They're not mine."
   186	
   187	"No. You've said." She set a strip of clean bark down on the scrubbed plank, and a pot of the hold's ink, and a pen cut from a goose quill. "There it is, all the same."
   188	
   189	Oryn looked at it for some time after the door had shut.
   190	
   191	She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
   192	
   193	She could not read an arm from here. She had said as much in a yard, of a nearer gate than this.
   194	
   195	So the arm would wait until she could have it between her two hands, and that would not be before the thaw. The girl knew who was to say when. There was nothing in that to write.
   196	
