editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:205:Lira was standing a pace above the iron, where she had stopped, with her right hand shut on the strap of the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:*Four sites. One line. The last one's twice the size and the Compact fenced it. The bearing runs through it and keeps going. Karis says four points is an argument. I say it's a bearing — the kind their maps draw as nothing. Home is Lowmarch now. I just wrote that without noticing. Nineteen, ten fragments, a still place at the center of me, and a line on a map that somebody drew before there were maps. We're not done. We're oriented.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:163:*Three. Deployment since the Line: twenty-six.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:301:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:25:The foot was taped, from a roll that had been put into her hand at a fork, without a word, by a person who had then walked north; and she wound it herself each morning, sitting on a box, with her one hand and her teeth. Then she set the great boot down whole at every stride of every mile, on ruts that were iron and on turf that was glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:109:He had not asked her to come. He had only told her what he had seen, and she had named the beast that she would load. It was how she had joined the crew in the first place, nine cold terms ago, at a ford with a carter's leg in it: she had looked at how the thing was being carried, and had put down her bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:31:*Hundred and twenty-ninth day, night. A back room with a door on it, in the lower inn at Lowmarch. First snow. Ninety-six in hand. Nine lines in Pike's book, and no number against the season. Eleventh on the cloth. Deployment since the Line: twenty-seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:103:The jenny went as she had always gone, which was badly. She led as a gate swings. For the whole of the first morning Oryn's right hand kept going out to a small brown cheek that was two days behind her on another road; it met a grey ear like an oar, and came back. She let it. She had told a girl in a yard that a beast should not be named, and had learned at a wicket how little that helped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:251:"Someone made this pattern."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:255:"I have ten things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no file I can see — and five people, one of them on a route, who've walked into the quiet three times and come out." He turned it over once and let it stand. "And a line on a map that somebody drew before there was a registry to draw one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:55:It was Brom who answered her, and he did it by going to the left-hand box for the bowls. He set out six on the turf between the two irons, as he had on every night since a grey stone on the Fallow; and the healer, who had told a girl at a mill that she would eat out of the sixth and not sit in a place, watched him do it and held her tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:131:"Gone," she said. She drew the foot back by the length of itself. "Back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:139:She had brought a stone down from the rise in both hands, grey, and as big as a loaf. She set it on the turf where Lira's toe had been, as she had set one on the Fallow and one between two bars of iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:1:# Chapter 59 — First Snow
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:145:"No. We've not forgotten it."
