editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:23:*The lone line is present. Divisions seen; not counted; not measured.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:25:She read that over, and let it stand. It was true, and it was less than she wanted, and she had learned from a man with apple trees to tell those two apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:35:She had her own reach for each of the three, in cords. What she wanted beside it was the other man's: the figure that a surveyor had gone over twice with his pencil, in a room under a floor, until it cut the paper. She had heard it read out once. She had written it on her cuff, in the dark of that room, and the cuff had since been in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:She understood that well enough. She had lived for years beside shelves that she was allowed to read at and forbidden to carry from. And he had been the keeper of one shut thing for twelve years; it was not to be supposed that he would take up a second and hold it open to everybody. He had come twenty yards across the turf with it, unasked, which was a great deal farther than she had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:It was entered. She sat and looked at what lay above it. There was a perimeter, three times, to the stride. There were forty-one kinds, and none strange. There was a line on a wall that was divided to a circle's radius, and stakes that were numbered in a row. And now there was a ruler lying through the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:109:"Thirty-one. Thirty-two. Thirty-three. They're in a row. I'm not saying what it counts; she'll not let me. I'm saying which way it counts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:He did it on purpose. He had counted that crew on a hundred roads without thinking; today he looked at each of them as he would have looked at a room that he was to leave in the morning. Seln went far out in front, small against the sky, with a case on his chest and a book in his coat. Karis walked with the hide rolled under her arm, and her lips moving. Lira had the healer's bow slung across her back on its strap, and she set the great boot down whole at every stride; she had been given no order to hand that bow back, and she had not offered. Brom led the brown mule on her low side, with the kit on his left shoulder and four yards of webbing holding the rest of him together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:171:"They'll not hire till the thaw," said Brom. "Nor feed a stranger. I asked him once what a crew does out here when the board shuts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:193:"I owe the rest of you something. She's had it, as she set it, so it's free." He kept it short. "There's a place in me, under the breastbone, where nothing moves. All ten fragments go round it. I've read it three times, and it keeps. I don't know what it is, and I've been told to stop touching it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:They did not go back as they had come. Seln had read the hide by Karis's lamp on the crest, and had found them a shorter way than a marsh and a stair: along the ridge to the heap of chock-stones, and then north by the hide carters' road, which ran through shut holds toward a river that all of them knew. He walked it ahead of them, as he walked everything. Four times in a day he came back along the file and said his word, and after the second day Karis left off writing down the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:15:On the fourth the wind went into the north and stayed there, and they walked with their chins in their collars and the mule's breath freezing on her whiskers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:25:The foot was taped, from a roll that had been put into her hand at a fork, without a word, by a person who had then walked north; and she wound it herself each morning, sitting on a box, with her one hand and her teeth. Then she set the great boot down whole at every stride of every mile, on ruts that were iron and on turf that was glass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:29:He was three strides behind. He saw the boot go, and her right arm fly out; and he saw the left, under the grey wool, start to come and stop at a knot. She went down on her right shoulder and her hip, hard, with the bow clattering on her back. She lay for one breath with her cheek on the ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:53:On the hundred and twenty-fourth night, the first of the road home, Seln read a page and a half that he had by heart already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:He read it on his watch, as he had on every night since a room under a floor. He sat with his back to four sleepers and the limp little book open on his knee, tilted to what the embers gave. He did not need the light. He used it regardless, since there is a difference between remembering a hand and looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:71:He had written *nothing to report* at the foot of a weekly return for twelve years, over the top of a case, and had thought it the hardest sentence in the service to write honestly. This man had written a better one. He had written what was there. He had put down eight figures that he did not understand, because they were so, and had underlined the one that frightened him; and Seln, who had read some thousands of returns, could not remember another that had been made by a person so little concerned with what his reader wished to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:It was three days later, on the fourth of that road, with the wind gone into the north, that he saw what he had been doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:81:He had not meant to keep it so. He had put it into his coat in a cold room because it was registry paper. Reading registry paper was the one craft that he had brought east with him. That had been true. It was also true that he had read it to nobody since. A researcher had wanted one figure out of it, at a fire by a marsh, and had not felt able to ask. She had stopped with her pencil over a leaf, and looked at her own cuff, where the figure had been washed out. He had gone to her. He had held the page to the light for her three times with his fingers shut on its spine.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:89:He had spent fifteen years learning to keep things from being read. He could think of no worse shelf for a dead man's true figures than the coat of such a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:131:It came on as they went down, thinly, out of no wind at all. Cael walked through the meadow to the river's edge, to the planks of the landing, and stopped there and counted what he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:It was the fifth time that he had read that town, and the first from below. He had always had it from a saddle or a ridge, laid out like a board; now it stood over him. The river went by at his feet, dark, between two shelves of ice that had grown since he saw them last until each was as broad as a cart is long. The ferry's cable sagged across from bank to bank with a white rime on it. The scow lay on the near side, and a man with a mallet was knocking ice from its running blocks. In the doorway of the round shed a brown gelding with one white foot stood under a blanket and watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:171:"So I'd heard." Pike dipped his pen, and wrote, and blew on it. In the last column, where a price went, he set down three things with a stop between each, and read them out as he made them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:He took it by its corner and swung it on the bench, so that the writing faced the other way. Cael had read every line of that book upside down for a hundred and twenty days, across an oak plank, as he had once read a bailiff's list on a counter. It lay the right way up before him now. He read the season in a square black hand, and at the end of it a ruled space with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:She turned to Lira, and held out her two hands, and Lira gave her the sling without a word. The wool came down. The healer's fingers went round the forearm, overlapping, and she took the short reading, to ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:Lira had the bow off her back. She held it out by the stock, with the one bolt lying in its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:There were four of them behind him, and one mule. They stood at the heap of stones as she had left them: a small man with a case on his chest, and a small woman with a hide under her arm, and a very big one bound up like a parcel, and a girl with a bow on her back and one sleeve empty. It was the crew that had come down off a shelf above a valley with no name, on the second day past the Line, and it looked a good deal the worse for wear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:Karis had it on the hide at the bend, with her back to the wind. The bright edge lay through her three dots and ran off the skin; and when she lifted her eyes from it, they went along the back of the ridge, which was long, and bare, and going exactly as the brass went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:She did not pretend that the line was better than it was. At each halt she had Seln stand a furlong ahead of her, and looked back along the turf to the last place where they had rested; then she moved him a few paces to one hand or the other, with her arm, until the two marks and her own boots lay in a row. It was how a person carries a ruler across country when she has none long enough, and she said that it was good to the width of the strip and no better.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:85:Lira walked beside him for a mile in the afternoon, on the frozen turf, with the bow knocking against her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:93:They slept on the ridge in a hollow, without wood, in every blanket that they had. Brom laid six bowls on the turf. He looked at the row, and took one back; and it passed without remark, since he had done exactly that on a hundred nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:101:By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:111:It was true, and he let it stand; and the other thing was in the same coat with it, and had been all morning. They were walking along a ruler. Three floors lay in a row, as stones lie along a path; and when he looked ahead down the ridge, to where the strip went on into the blue, he was more afraid of what was under the brass than he had been of a thing in a marsh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:123:The wind had dropped with the sun. There was no bird over that ground, and no beast on it, and nothing that moved in all the width of it but the last of the light going up the far slope; Cael had stood above two such places, and neither had been a third the size of this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Below the ruts, a furlong down the slope, there was something else going round. It was a line of small dark uprights, set close, running away on both hands with the curve of the ground until the dusk had it. From the crest it looked like a row of stitches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:159:Nobody crossed. Cael came down to her, and the others behind him; and they stood in a row along the outside of that fence, in the last of the light, as people stand at the rail of a bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:205:Lira was standing a pace above the iron, where she had stopped, with her right hand shut on the strap of the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:219:"Not without her. Not without knowing what a circle twice the size does to the four of you. And not without the map finished."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:They climbed back to the crest and slept there, in the wind, with the valley black below them and nothing lit in it. He wrote by her lamp, when the others were down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:21:"Five, on the track," he said. "They are in file, a crew's length apart, with two mules. One has a spear. One favours a leg. The fifth I do not know; it is a woman, not young, who carries a staff and does not lean on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:He went over it as he went over a load. Six people had lain round that low ground for a day with their faces covered, and had gone off it north and south and west when a boy stood up with a light. They had gone as people go who have practised leaving. He had read where they lay, by lantern, and had found nothing above the height of a boot. People who take such pains over what they leave behind are not people who go home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:89:"For them," said the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:105:She had put her spoon down. He knew her opinion, since she had given it to him over the bow's hip more times than he could count. A thing put right in an hour by a Path was a thing that nobody afterward thought to tie. Hands were quick, she said, and quick was not the same as held.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:117:It kept a road for the first of them, and a grown-over track for the second, and he read the six as he went. The big one was carrying on his left side. The girl was setting her right foot down whole, at every stride, and had been told to. The small one stopped at each halting-place and knelt, and laid something on her knee; and the quiet one, whom he had needed two looks to find in an inn, had been out in front of the rest the whole way, alone. On the third morning the track gave out, and the trail did not so much as waver. That was what he could not get past. People go to water, or to a roof, or along whatever road there is. These had gone over wet grass with no mark to steer by, round nothing, toward nothing that he could see, as a plumb line hangs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:123:"Well?" the bow had said, when he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:135:There was a camp at the track's end with two fires. He counted, as he counted before he did anything, and he counted his own first: a spear, and a bow, and a boy, and an old woman with a board. Then he counted the camp, and there were six in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:143:He had told himself at a grey stone that a captain who asks a question he has no column for has only given himself something more to carry. It had been sound, and he had had three to carry then. He had four now. And he was proposing to stand on some floor beside that boy after the thaw, if the boy went where he seemed to be going. A man does not walk his people up to a thing that he has been too careful to ask the name of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:165:The tenth did not stir, since it never asked for anything. But he knew what was within his reach, as a man with a key in his pocket knows a lock when he passes one. It would take two fingers, and a count of ten. At three he would have this man's banks under his hand, and at ten he would know the Path as he knew a friend's step. In a minute more he would have what ran in it, and how deep. That was the Bronze which a one-armed man had guessed at under his eave, and had never been told. A registrar would have given a year's pay for what lay a foot from his fingers. He could have had the whole of it and told nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:169:Nor did he ask whether he might, and he saw why as he sat there. There was only one person whose leave could be asked, and that was the man himself; and to ask him was to ask that he hand over, to a boy at a fire, what he had kept out of every book that was ever opened on him. A healer had told Cael at a well that asking was the least of it. A hand went onto a person when there was something to read *for*. He had nothing to read this man for but the wish to know what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:189:"I don't have one. Neither does the board. You said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:203:*Teague sat down next to me. Asked what I am. I could have read him. I asked instead. The day I stop asking is the day I'm the registry. Write it down. Again. Every time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:She laid them in three heaps along the deal, and did not try to make their edges agree; the Fallow's were long strips, and the Stair's were leaves, and the hall's were whatever a person could tear from a roll with her teeth. There were eleven in the first heap. There were twenty-nine in the second, and twelve in the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:53:He sorted it aloud. He had done so with every bag that Cael had seen come over, each piece given out twice, so that people who could not read should know what was theirs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:69:He read it standing in the snow, and then he read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:83:*Vell. There's a second ledger on the continent with my name in it, kept by a one-armed man in a town on no map, and this week he stopped numbering me. He said his book was wrong about us by being too small. You said once that somebody should have a record that doesn't answer to the Compact. There are two now. The session still stands. — C.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:89:"It will say that a crew kept what it was sent for," he said. "It cannot say why, since he does not know. I find that I can bear that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:She led the jenny in under the gate-walk. A lean man stood up there with a long bow at the length of his arm, as he had stood on every evening that she could remember, and he lifted two fingers from the stave. She had never known him do that for her. She understood, crossing the yard, that it was not for her: it was for a girl with one arm, who had sat on those planks beside him and shot at a sack, and he was asking after her in the only way that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:"He goes down to the board at first light, with my bark. He'll not come up again this side of the thaw, if that does what it looks like." The woman jerked her chin at the north. "If you've anything to send to that crew of yours, it's tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:She could not read an arm from here. She had said as much in a yard, of a nearer gate than this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:225:It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:227:Nobody spoke for a breath. Brom had six bowls in a row on the board. He looked along them. He had put the last one down a little apart, as he always did; and he did not take it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:265:Nothing out here had read him. At three floors older than any registry, every Path in the party had stopped at a line and his had not; a fourth had been paced from outside and not crossed. Nobody alive — least of all him — knew why. Someone had made the pattern. That was as far as anyone could say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:7:"Down. On the left side. Somebody get that coat off him, and somebody light me a fire, and I don't care which of you does which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:23:Lira held nothing; she sat on the grey stone with the bow across her knee, and looked from the healer's hands to the reeds, and back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:57:And his eyes would not hold a near thing. That was the read, which he had leaned on harder than ever in his life, through water and stone, and when he looked at his own hand it swam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:79:"Lie still. I want to read you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:"I know what I'll get. I've had it six times." She was counting already, under the words. "I've had my hands on every one of them this morning, to see what that place had done. I'll not sit down to my bread with one left out, only because he can't be read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:95:"They're not gone again; you'd not have walked that path. They've been leaned on, by a thing that I'd not like to put a weight to." She sat back on her heels. "I can't shut what I can't read. You've heard me say it till you're sick of it. That's ten days of knowing that you've ribs, and they'll talk when it turns cold, and they'll not do more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:"I told you a night that's only a night, and I meant one with nothing in front of it. You've not had one since, and you'll not get one on this road. But I've been looking at you for a quarter of an hour, and there's another kind." She nodded at his chest. "You read it frightened, in a loft. Tonight there's nothing left in you to be frightened with; I doubt that you could be afraid of a wolf. So you'll read it tonight, when the rest are down. Then you'll sleep, and you'll read it once more when you wake, before you've had time to think. That's three, on three days, with a night between each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:121:Lira had not left the grey stone. She sat on it with her foot set down whole and the healer's bow along her knee, with its nose toward the reeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:125:"Seln says that a night won't hurt it." She did not turn round. "There's one. If I let it down, I've a stick with a string on, and nobody here to bend it who isn't asleep or mending. It stays in the bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:127:"It's not coming out of that water. He read the mud."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:129:"I know what he read. I watched it come up out of a hole this morning, the whole of it, when a carter had said that nobody ever saw so much." Her right hand moved on the stock, a finger's breadth, and lay still. "I'm not saying that it's coming. I'm saying that I've one, and it's in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:131:He left her to it, since she had sat on a stair for a day once with that same bow, for the same reason, and had been right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:155:*Hundred and nineteenth day. The track's end, outside the stone. All six out. Brom's shoulder opened on a wall and is mended a second time, an hour; it will not be mended a third. Four bolts spent; one in the bow, with Lira.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:*The read: the floor, through water and a plate. All of it, for three counts of warning.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:169:He read himself that night when the others were down, by her order, and he was an hour about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:At ten the short reading gave him his own gait, quick and slow, and tonight a good deal more slow than quick. He went on beyond ten, into the deep reading, and it was easier than it had been in a loft, since he had nothing in him tonight that could hurry it; he went along the dry hiss of the Wind at a walk, looking for a side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:177:He knew now what he was looking for. He had been in a river with banks, on a ridge, for a minute, and had felt them stand close and high on either hand like the walls of a mill-race. He went the whole length of the Wind and found none. The millstone was under it, and ran into it, and he could not have said where; he tried the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, which was thinner tonight than he had ever heard it, and they were ten, and not one of them had an edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:He had stood on a wall that morning and looked down at a spring. The water there had come up against a fallen slab and piled, and curled back behind it in a slow eddy, as water does when it meets a thing lying in its road. He looked for the piling now, and there was none. Not one of the ten came up against that place. Each of them began to lean a long way short of it, and came back to its line a long way past; they went by it as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river, without a ripple, along a way that was worn smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:209:He woke while the stars were still out, with frost on his blanket, and read it a third time before he had fairly remembered where he was. He was rested, and cold, and clear in the head, which was a third temper and as unlike the loft as it was unlike the night before; and the place lay where it had lain, with the ten going round it by the same worn way. It took him less than a quarter of an hour, and he wrote one line afterward by feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:227:"You're going to tell me about a thing that I'll never get my hands on. I'd take a finding from a second healer across a table, if I had felt the patient. I've not felt this one. I've had a river in the dark off you seven times, and that's all that I'll ever have." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "But you were in me for a minute, on that ridge, and I felt you go along. I know your step. If you're in me while you say it, I'll have the thing that found it, under my own skin, while it tells me what it found. It's the nearest that I'll come."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:231:"No. You'll be saying *no banks* with a bank under your hand, and *doesn't move* with something that does. A person says a thing truer when the other kind is in front of him." She pushed back her left cuff. "It's my wrist. You know what comes next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:235:"I'm asking, then. Will you let me read you, now, while I tell it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:237:"Yes. Hand on the wrist. The short reading first, to ten, and then the neck, and the long reading for one minute, which I'll count. You'll talk while you're in. If I say *out*, you come out with the sentence in your mouth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:239:He put his right hand on her wrist, which was broad, and cold, with the white scar across the heel of the hand; and the tenth turned toward it, and went. At three there were the banks, close and high, and at ten he knew her, as a person knows a step on a stair. He laid his left palm on the back of her neck, under the cropped hair, and she let out one long breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:243:He was in, with the single bed about him, and the one deep thing in it, running. It was the strangest place in which he had ever tried to tell the truth: he went along a healer's river at his own uneven pace, with her banks standing close on either hand, and said aloud to her what the same going-along had found three times in a body that had none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:279:"I don't know what right would look like in you. Nobody does. That's not a comfort; it's a finding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:291:"It isn't a wound."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:293:"I don't know what it is, and nor do you. I know what a person does with a place in himself that he's found: he goes back to it every night with his fingers." She was already turning toward Brom's blankets. "You've looked three times. You know where it is, and that it keeps. Let it keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:15:They lay thick in that corner, wavy and close, as he had drawn them nowhere else on the skin. In the midst of them was the round of bare hide that Karis had ringed on the ninety-second evening, with a word beside it. The edge went by that ring, on the side toward the corner, and there was the width of a finger between the brass and her pencilled round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:29:Seln had been reading the hide over her shoulder, as he read any paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:149:"I shall enter the figure," said Karis. "And that you said, in a barn, that one does not stamp a bar thirty-one and stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:193:"We learned what the Fallow did to each of us before we walked a furlong into it," she said. "I should like to know what this one does before I kneel down in it with my back turned."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:275:She gave it to him before she had thought, and then she stared, and so did the rest of them. He had read out a great many things to that notebook in a hundred days: the width of a road, and the age of a bar, and what a paper meant. He had asked each time that it should be entered as said. He had never once put a mark in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:277:He wrote four short lines, in the small square hand of a man who had filled other people's files for fifteen years, and read them over as he read anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:279:"It's blind. It reads weight on the floor. It strikes where the weight is. And it strikes what *stays*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:289:"A stone stays because it's a stone," he said. "What does it do with a thing that stays on purpose?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:325:"It reads that as the heaviest thing on the floor," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:327:"It reads that as the thing that *stays*," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:339:"In a minute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:341:"Then have your minute. Nobody else is to stop when we go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:349:They made their camp at the track's end, a few yards outside the grey stone, with the two mules hobbled on the rise. It had come on cold again with the dusk. Seln lit the fire, and the six of them sat close about it while Karis said what she wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:351:"The walls are cut on the inner side. I could see the courses through the gap, and I could not have read one stroke of them at thirty yards if I had sat on that kerb for a week." She had her hands round her knees. "So it is the inside or it is nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:353:"There's a way to do it," said Seln. "It has given us its rule, and a rule is a plan. On the floor, we move. We rest only on what comes up out of the old work: the path, and the kerb, and the tops of those walls, which are a yard through. A wall-top is a kerb set on end." He counted it off. "We go in at first light. Lira carries the bow, spanned, since she can loose it and cannot bend it. I carry the hook and the sleeve. Karis has her paper and nothing else. Thirty yards of plates, at a walk, with nobody standing; in at the gap; and up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:371:Lira had the healer's bow across her knee, and she was looking past the fire at the dark place where the reeds began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:5:Seln stepped over the grey stone first. Lira went second, with the healer's bow hanging from her right hand by its stock, spanned, and one of the five bolts held in the groove under her thumb. Karis had a roll of thin paper under one arm and her pencils in her hair. Cael walked fourth, and Brom behind him, with nothing on his back for the first time in three days and his right arm bound across his chest. The healer came last, with a pannier on her shoulder and her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:17:The water came to the middle of his shin. He did not stand in it; he went straight on, at the pace of a man crossing a yard, and the four of them went down after him one by one. The plates were slick under Cael's boots, and each of them gave a little as his weight came onto it, and rocked back as he left it, so that it was like walking over the lids of a row of great chests. He counted his own strides. He did not let any one of them be longer than its fellow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:"Left," said Seln. "The low place. I go up, and I take the bow from her, and then her hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:33:He went at the first of the breaks without slackening, and put both palms on the round of it, and was sitting on the wall's top before Cael had seen how. Lira held the bow up to him by its stock. He laid it behind him, and took her right hand in both of his, and she walked up the cut face of the wall on her whole sole as though it had been a stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:35:Karis came to the foot of the break with the roll under her arm, and reached up. She was a head shorter than Lira. Her fingers went over the round of the stone, and found nothing to close on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:61:He fell along the tipping plate toward the gap they had come in by, on his left arm, and rolled. Where he had stood there was a darkness as broad as a door, and the two halves of it came together on a yard of water and the skirt of his coat. Cael saw the cloth tear. He saw a head as blunt as the bow of a skiff, the colour of mud, and no eye in it anywhere. Then it had gone down, and the plate dropped back, and Brom was up on his knees in the wash of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:63:"It's blind. It reads the floor. Get *off* the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:75:Nobody moved, and for the space of ten breaths the only sounds in that place were the spring, and five people getting their wind, and the small knocking going round below them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:79:"Karis," said Lira. "Here; I've the bow. Cael's along from us, at the corner. You're across the hole. Brom?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:85:Cael looked at where each of them was, since there was nothing else that he could do for them. Karis and Lira sat on the northern wall near its low end, with the bow between them. Seln had gone along the same wall-top at a crouch, past Cael, and round the corner, and stood now on the eastern wall, which was the tallest, where he could see the whole of the hole. Cael was at the corner between them. And Brom lay by himself on the far side of the water, on a strip of wall no longer than a cart, which ended at the gap on one hand and at nothing on the other. Its top was green, and wet, since the spring's water slopped over it on the way out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:119:"No, I can see that you're not," said Lira; and she laid the bow down on the stone, and took a fistful of Karis's belt in her right hand, and held on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:127:Brom had got himself up to sitting. He sat astride the low wall as a man sits a horse, with his bound arm against his chest, and he was studying the gap beside him. Cael could read what was in his face. The gap was two strides wide. On its far side the wall began again, low, and ran on to the corner, and round, to where the others were; and at the foot of the gap, between the two ends of wall, lay the sill. It was the old work. A man who stood on it stood on nothing that the thing below could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:145:It shot from under him on the wet, and Brom's body did what any body does. His right arm came out of its binding. It caught the wall's end above his head and took the whole of him, on a shoulder that a healer had shut five days ago and given a week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:177:Lira had the bow up, and nothing to loose it at but water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:191:He gave the read the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:231:It came after the slab as it had come after a stone, and the slab stayed. He felt the jaws close on the place. Everything that he had went thin. He was on his hands and knees on a plate with water to his elbows, and he could not have said which way the wall was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:233:A hand had him by the collar, and another under his arm. He was dragged up a face of cut stone like a wet sack, with his ribs shrieking, and laid along the top of it. Seln's voice, close to his ear, said that he was to keep hold of whatever he was holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:253:She had slid down from her knees to the seat of her breeches on the wall-top, with her left leg folded under her and her right knee drawn up. The sole of the great boot was planted whole on the stone. Behind her right shoulder a length of the wall's old coping still stood, a foot higher than the rest. She set her shoulder against it, and laid the stock across her knee, and drew its butt back into the hollow of that shoulder. The knee and the coping held the bow between them, and her right hand had only a lever to mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:257:Her left arm lay where a healer had tied it, high against the collar-bone, and did not stir. A lean man had told her on a gate-walk that a bow wants one hand, and a thing that does not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:269:Seln had the bow out of her hand before the word was finished. He stood up on a yard of stone, with his boot in the iron and the hook on the string, and straightened his back; the string came up the stock and dropped. He laid a bolt in the groove from the sleeve at his belt, and put the bow into her hand again, and she had not once moved her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:279:Seln had the bow again, spanned, with the third bolt in it. He had been holding it out to her. He did not answer; he went along the wall-top to the corner at a run, bent double, and round it onto the eastern wall, and Cael saw him bring a crossbow to his shoulder for the first time in two years of knowing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:303:"It is alive," said Seln. He was coming back along the wall with the bow. "And it is going down, and it is hurt, and I do not know how long any of those will last. Karis. Paper. We walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:315:On the kerb, which was the hall's outer coping, Seln stopped, since a person could. He set his boot in the stirrup once more, and spanned, and laid the last bolt in the groove; and he held the bow out to Lira by its stock.

