## New canon minted (flag where marked)

- **The brass on the hide (flag).** The Ternhall strip is slid down along itself with a pencil dot (a ruler "as long as its owner is patient"). Laid through the Fallow's ring and the Stair's, it runs into the carter's water-strokes and passes his third blank **a finger outside** Karis's pencilled ring. She moves nothing on the 115th. On the 120th she rubs the ring out and draws it where the cord put it, with *moved; paced.* On the 123rd she adds a fourth ring "at the very edge of the skin", with no word beside it; by the 131st a fine pencil line is ruled through the three centres and the fourth ring and off the hide. **What a ruler on a map of days is worth:** "a direction that is good to the breadth of this pencil at the length of my arm… a strip of country a mile wide." On the ridge she carries the line by ranging: Seln a furlong ahead, the last resting-place behind, moved until the three lie in a row. No compass direction is claimed for the line as a whole (ruled KEEP).
- **What the carter said of the corner:** that he had seen it once, from a hill, with water standing in it, and never had a wheel within a day of it.
- **The road to the Hall (flag):** the Stair road beyond the low ground, south and east under the ridge, for a day; then a grown-over track with a tree in one rut, for a day; then no track for some hours; then the track's last furlong straight down to the reeds. A mild spell on the 116th–118th; hard frost again from the night of the 120th.
- **The Drowned Hall's ground (flag; one picture for chapters 53–56):** a basin "as round as a plate"; reeds and black water to the line and no farther (the same perimeter as the other two, sixty-two stations, by cord on the 120th); a causeway of the old grey, a stride wide and a hand above the water, **a hundred and eighty-five yards** from the grey stone to the kerb; **the kerb, "the hall's outer coping"**, two strides broad round a dish seventy yards across, holding a foot or two of clear moving water over a floor broken into great plates with round edges; in the middle the hall, twelve strides square, walls a yard through, three times Brom's height at one corner and chest-high in two places, with lengths of an old coping still standing on the wall-tops, cut on the inner face in courses ("nine deep where the wall was whole"); one gap with a sill of the grey; inside, a ledge three or four strides broad round a breach "as wide as a cart is long", where the spring stands up in a low dome; one great slab aslant in the breach (it slides down into the depth when the point is let go).
- **Stake thirty-three (flag):** one, at the track's end, a boot's width from Karis's stone; scaled in leaves like the old ones; the registry's stamp and two figures, "and nothing beneath them". Seln: five cold terms, "of an age with the Fallow's, and with the old one at our last stone… the same mallet drove all three"; no second. **A second stake on the kerb**, a few yards from the causeway's head, bent over at the height of a knee "like a nail that's been struck from underneath"; not examined closely and not numbered on the page.
- **The mud:** long low ridges as broad as a cart's bed, a yard or two inside the reeds, heaved from below; one old, one made since the frost went; none comes onto the grass.
- **The wold-wyrm (flag).** Oryn's word, from a salt carter with a crushed foot whom she mended at the mill "two rounds back"; he called the place the drowned hall. As seen: a blunt head "like the bow of a skiff", mud-brown, smooth, seamed down the middle where its two halves meet, with no eye; a back as broad as the ledge; longer than the room by twice; pale as tallow beneath. It lives under the marsh and under the plates, and has never been out of the line. **Its rule, as tried:** it goes its round under the dish about once in a quarter of an hour; a stone left lying is taken at a count of eleven; a skipped stone is followed and taken where it stops, at fourteen; people walking the causeway are paced and not struck; a standing man is struck at three, and once roused it stays in the room and strikes a dropped pebble at four. It is hit four times in the pale and goes down alive.
- **Anchor, as first set (flag):** by contact only, his palm on the surface; it "went down his arm as a plumb line goes down a well, and there was a point under his hand"; it stays when the hand is taken away, for as long as he holds it; he can see it at arm's length and feel it "as he would have felt his own finger kept upon a line of a page". A held plate cannot be tipped. Held against a biting thing, it takes everything else that he has. No range beyond contact, no rate and no limit of time is assigned. **The first point he ever sets is the trial of the 118th, which his log does not count as a deployment.**
- **Compression in the Hall:** the strike goes "through the bones of his legs, and his hips, and up his back and down again, and out through both heels into the ledge"; the tipping plate is driven flat.
- **The fight's ledger:** Brom thrown toward the gap and onto the low wall beside it; about an hour on the walls; his right arm out of its binding as his boot slides; Oryn across thirty yards of plates, hopping, never two counts on one; Karis's cheek scraped raw on the wall's edge (washed, not mended). **Lira's two shots are one-handed:** seated on the wall-top, the great boot planted whole, the stock across her drawn-up right knee and its butt in the hollow of her right shoulder, which is set against a standing length of the wall's old coping; her left arm lies in its sling and does not stir. Seln spans standing on the wall; his two are from the eastern wall, the second held a hand high since the bow throws low. Karis has twelve sheets, all from the inner face of the one wall that she lay on.
- **Oryn at the marsh:** the second mending takes an hour, with her wet to the waist in the frost and shaking; she sleeps sitting up among them that afternoon, with Brom's wrist in her hand; her reading of Cael at the stake is the seventh.
- **Two fires:** the near, for wet people; the far, twenty yards upwind and "no bigger than a hat", for paper.
- **The second and third self-readings (flag):** the second on the night of the 119th, about an hour: no banks, known now for what a bank is; the place where it was; and a **fourth countable thing**: none of the ten piles against it or eddies behind it; each begins to lean a long way short and comes back a long way past, "as a river goes round a stone that has lain in its bed for as long as there has been a river". The third before light on the 120th, under a quarter of an hour: the same. Oryn's three tempers: frightened (the loft), spent (the marsh), rested.
- **The telling:** his right hand on her wrist for the short reading, his left on the back of her neck for the minute; he speaks while he is in; she counts twenty, forty, sixty. Her reasons for "You in me." are two: she will have "the thing that found it, under my own skin, while it tells me what it found", and he will say *no banks* with a bank under his hand. She says that his step did not alter.
- **Karis's leaf of the three places (flag):** one leaf made four times: one for each box and the first in her coat with the trials leaf. On it: the three names; perimeter and radius; the stake numbers; the sheets (eleven, twenty-nine, twelve: fifty-two); *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more*; *The lone line is present. Divisions seen; not counted; not measured.*; the surveyor's *Reach, edge to centre* figure, copied from the book held open by Seln; *The three middles lie on one straight edge, as near as this skin can show.* One line of the leaf is left clean. She tried to make the brass miss and could not; she had laid the ruler herself, before the third place was known.
- **Teague's healer (flag; unnamed):** "sixty, or near it, as lean as a rake", grey hair knotted under a cap, a much-mended satchel, a staff that she does not lean on; no Path that mends ("I've no Path that knits"); forty cold terms of binding; with the crew for nine, since "a ford with a carter's leg in it"; she spends the dry months at her daughter's hold up the north water and comes down at the first hard frost. She carries a splinting board, four yards of webbing and raw wool. **The binding:** a pad under the arm; webbing round the chest, over the point of the shoulder and back beneath the elbow, three times, with "a drover's" turn (under, and back on itself) that keeps the weight on the ribs and off the joint. Oryn asks to be shown it twice.
- **Teague, days 115–121 (flag):** **he did not set the weir's third stake; the ford-keeper did not mark the shingle; the four marks were lost, as Movement 7 closed it.** He sent word to Pike by the mill's carter ("five strokes to look at, and all of them walking; and the three hundred was to stay where it lay"); followed the trail from the Stair for three days "for what's behind them, if there's anything", walking back a mile along it at each dusk; found nobody on it. He hears Karis's sentence and Cael's ruling on the 121st, watches the brass run off the hide, and asks nothing. His five reach Lowmarch before the five do.
- **Seln's book-keeping:** the ledger was in his coat from the 115th to the 127th. In his window: no name in it; "a field ledger is signed for at a counter, and the name stays at the counter"; his own issue sheet read *Returned or accounted for*. He gives it to Karis on the fourth day of the road home with "It is a field book. It ought to lie with the papers of the field." She will enter where it was found, when, and by whose hand it came to her.
- **The road beyond the Hall (flag):** a made road an hour east of the marsh, with holds on it (two shut for the cold term, a third long dead, with one gable standing); the hide carters use it; it forks at a heap of chock-stones about a day on. The left hand climbs to a notch between two hills and is the gap to Thornwater, four days, coming down onto the Fallow road and in by Thornwater's eastern gate. The right hand goes on under a long bare ridge, "a great whale's back", a mile broad, which ends in a crest above the fourth site.
- **The fourth site, as seen (flag): OUTSIDE ONLY.** From the crest at dusk on the 123rd: "a great shallow head of pale ground, scooped out of the hills… and open toward the south"; no bird or beast; in the middle a thing "grey, and upright… taller than a man", with "a top that was not level" (Lira: "It's not one thing, I think. Or it is, and there's sky in it. I'd be guessing."). Two faint old ruts, with turf in them, go round the valley's head high up. **The cordon** lies about a hundred and ninety paces below those ruts: bars of the registry's pattern, one to a pace, each stamped *thirty-four* with the stroke and the bar beneath; Lira counts forty to the left and Brom forty to the right, and it goes on. **Lira stops one pace above the iron, at about a hundred and eighty-nine paces; her sigil is not tried; nobody says "Gone" or "Back"; nobody touches a stake; nobody crosses.** Cael "knew only that he had crossed three such lines before and that the fragments had answered there; nobody had tested this line, and nobody would." Seln puts no date on the cordon and nobody assigns a purpose to whoever set it. Karis lays the long cord twenty times along the feet of the bars, from outside: the short measure comes to half of what it has always come to. Her notebook's sum: **four hundred and forty yards, and two strides**. What the line does to a Path is unknown; Lira's question stands unanswered. Seln: "There was a sheet with two… A marsh three days behind us, and this: I can place it now."
- **Pike's first snow (flag; ruled KEEP):** "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw." He has done forty that day. The ninth line is given in narration (a recovery, on the Stair road, at a broker's posting; taken; six gone in; instruments found, brought up and kept by the crew that found them; six come out). The clerk "has been up my bank four times to ask what he's to put a mark to", and is given nothing. Pike turns the ledger toward Cael for the first time in this edition.
- **The inn (flag):** the lower inn's keeper is "a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder". The back room is "the store behind the chimney": narrow, one small window, a deal table, meal-sacks, warm from the back of the hearth. Six marks for the cold term; the five may lie at the hearth's end "for the standing-money… as long as there's paper in my store". A small window at the far end of the hearth looks down on the landing. **On the table:** the kit's case in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid; inside it a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point (never driven), and a mallet; beside it the dead man's ledger, shut; the three boxes against the warm wall; fifty-two sheets in three heaps.
- **The fence:** "the long one, east, with the axe-head on its gate"; a day's posts still to set and the whole far side to mend before the thaw; "A hold will feed a crew that's mending its fence."
- **The last ferry (flag):** on the 130th, at noon, through a lane cut in the shelf ice; the post comes down to the far bank with the courier of day 4 (the hat with one side cut away). **Nine pieces:** the smith; the upper inn (a bill); two for the landing broker under his own seal; the hide-woman; a hold at the top of the north water; Teague's healer, from her daughter; Thornwater; and Hesk's parcel ("From a town I never heard of"). Hesk's straightedge is "a hand longer than the one from Ternhall, and a little broader". Cael's two letters go back by the same boat, with the clerk's packet. The ferryman and his boy take the hauling rope off the drum and coil it in the shed; the gelding goes to the smith's stable. The rope is still owed: "I'll not want it this side of the thaw."
- **Oryn, days 122–128 (flag):** four days through the gap with the jenny; Thornwater at dusk on the 125th; the bowman's two fingers, for Lira. **The old woman of the hurdle winters at Thornwater, at her daughter's hearth; Oryn had told her at the mill, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee there after the thaw.** She rested it ten days, as bid, and then walked the day and a half over the low place in two, with a grandson to carry the bread; she is first on the bench on the 126th; Oryn reads the knee (mended at Millrace on the 113th) and it has held. Seven on the bench, two mendings. Word by the stooped carter on the 127th: the mill a crushed thumb; two gates no burials; at the ford a finger lost to a chain and the hand kept; the child's breathing no different. On the evening of the 128th the steading's woman gives her bark, ink and a quill; the carter goes down at first light on the 129th and will not come up again before the thaw. She means to go north by the wicket when the bench is paid.
- **Roles (all unnamed):** Teague's healer; the hide carter (a scarf over his nose); the inn's keeper; the ferryman and his boy; the courier; the old woman and one grandson; the stooped carter; the Thornwater carter.

